course: dictionary
lesson: 8066
title: Same word, different meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show you which one the writer means. Relaxed is a good example. It can describe a feeling, a place, or a rule. «A loose, wiggly body and a soft, open mouth usually mean a relaxed, friendly dog.» Here relaxed means calm and not tense, because the dog looks loose and happy.
explain: «They pick a relaxed, ordinary place for the first meeting.» A place cannot feel calm, so relaxed here means easy-going, with no fuss and no stiff rules. Ask yourself what the word describes: a person, a place or a rule? That question often gives you the meaning.
explain: Relaxed can also be the past of relax. To relax means to rest, to become calm, or to make your muscles less tight, so relaxed means you did one of those things. Stretching in PE works better when your muscles are relaxed.
fact: Relaxed means calm and not worried or tense.
fact: A relaxed place or rule is easy-going and not strict.
fact: Relaxed is the past of relax: to rest, become calm, or make your muscles less tight.
fact: You can say «feel relaxed», «relaxed atmosphere» and «relaxed about» something.
fact: Context tells you which meaning a word has, so check the words around it.
exercise: «A loose, wiggly body and a soft, open mouth usually mean a relaxed, friendly dog.» What does relaxed mean here? | Tired and hungry | Fast and noisy | Strict and serious | *Calm and not tense
exercise: «They pick a relaxed, ordinary place for the first meeting.» What does relaxed describe here? | *A place that is easy-going and not formal | A place full of loud music | A place that is far away | A place that is very cold
exercise: «Some cinemas have "relaxed screenings", with quieter sound and the lights not fully off.» What are these screenings like? | *Easy-going, with quieter sound and lights not fully off | Only for teachers and grown-ups | Very loud, with bright lights | Silent, with no films at all
test: A child takes slow, deep breaths and stops worrying about her test. Which word describes her now? | barely | ancient | *relaxed | stressed
test: What does a relaxed rule mean? | Written in red ink | Very long and hard to follow | *Easy-going and not strict | Broken and no use to anyone
exam: Read: «Grown-ups usually wait until the relationship is serious before the children meet the new partner, and they pick a relaxed, ordinary place for the first meeting.» Which meaning of relaxed fits best here? | *Easy-going and not formal, with no fuss | Very loud and crowded | Tired after a long walk | Stretched out flat on the floor
